WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.302
Hi, I'm Pemberton!

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:05.676
Most open models without full attention now work this way.

00:00:05.676 --> 00:00:06.351
Here's how.

00:00:10.025 --> 00:00:13.202
Dense attention reads every cached token, for every new one.

00:00:13.622 --> 00:00:17.602
Index-selected attention scores them all cheaply, then reads only the best

00:00:17.602 --> 00:00:19.050
two thousand and forty-eight.

00:00:20.508 --> 00:00:24.389
The new token goes to the indexer, a second, much smaller attention.

00:00:24.809 --> 00:00:28.626
It scores every cached token with a ReLU, and keeps the top scores.

00:00:29.046 --> 00:00:32.394
Those positions point into the cache, which still holds everything.

00:00:32.814 --> 00:00:37.434
The main attention gathers just those entries and runs a normal softmax over them.

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:42.044
The score for one past token is a weighted sum over

00:00:42.044 --> 00:00:43.127
a few indexer heads.

00:00:43.547 --> 00:00:46.935
Each head dots its own small query with that token's index key.

00:00:47.355 --> 00:00:49.003
A ReLU replaces the softmax.

00:00:49.003 --> 00:00:50.980
The paper chose it for speed.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.163
And a weight per head, also from the query, mixes them.

00:00:55.825 --> 00:00:58.626
In DeepSeek's figure, the indexer is the green path.

00:00:58.626 --> 00:01:02.050
It feeds a top-k selector in front of the core attention.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:06.209
It sits on latent attention, so one selection per token serves every

00:01:06.209 --> 00:01:08.413
head, and the gather stays one list.

00:01:09.725 --> 00:01:12.540
The indexer is new weights on a trained model.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.103
First, everything else is frozen.

00:01:14.503 --> 00:01:18.115
It learns to match what dense attention would have looked at, summed over heads.

00:01:18.514 --> 00:01:21.912
Then selection switches on, and the whole model trains to live with it.

00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:25.013
The payoff is in decoding.

00:01:25.013 --> 00:01:26.659
Dense cost climbs with position.

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:29.621
The sparse model stays nearly flat to the end.

00:01:31.092 --> 00:01:33.117
What it does not save is storage.

00:01:33.117 --> 00:01:36.298
Nothing is evicted, so the cache is even a little bigger.

00:01:36.298 --> 00:01:38.323
What drops is what each token reads.

00:01:39.425 --> 00:01:41.685
And the scan still touches every token.

00:01:41.685 --> 00:01:45.236
At ten million tokens, the scorer is almost the whole layer.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.693
Keep every token, and read only the ones a cheap scorer ranks highest.

00:01:53.317 --> 00:01:55.826
Score everything, read a little, and keep it all.

00:01:55.826 --> 00:01:56.663
Thanks for watching!

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:00.167
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:00.809
I'm Pemberton.

00:02:00.809 --> 00:02:01.131
Bye!
